# Timeline JSON

The AI should output semantic editing decisions, not complex FFmpeg syntax:

```json
{
  "video": {
    "title": "Corbin — The World Between Worlds",
    "width": 1280,
    "height": 720,
    "fps": 25
  },
  "audio": {
    "narration": "audio/corbin_narration.wav",
    "ambient_bed": "audio/ambience/low_wind_01.wav",
    "ambient_gain_db": -28
  },
  "events": [
    {
      "type": "black",
      "start": 0,
      "duration": 2.5
    },
    {
      "type": "quote",
      "start": 2.5,
      "duration": 5,
      "text": "Where do images exist?",
      "font_role": "editorial_serif",
      "transition_in": "fade",
      "transition_out": "fade"
    },
    {
      "type": "visual",
      "start": 7.5,
      "duration": 14,
      "asset_id": "visual_door_mist_001",
      "treatment": "golden_imaginal",
      "entry": "fade_from_black",
      "exit": "fade_to_black"
    },
    {
      "type": "black",
      "start": 21.5,
      "duration": 2
    },
    {
      "type": "artwork",
      "start": 23.5,
      "duration": 16,
      "asset_id": "art_corbin_003",
      "presentation": "gallery_white",
      "motion": "slow_push",
      "entry": "fade"
    }
  ]
}
```

Python then resolves:

* asset IDs into paths;
* named presets into FFmpeg filters;
* font roles into installed font paths;
* event times into filtergraph instructions.

